course: everyday
lesson: 91
title: Worry and sadness
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Worry is the feeling of thinking again and again about something that might go wrong; a little worry helps you prepare, but too much worry makes rare things feel certain and spoils sleep and fun.
explain: Worries shrink when they leave your head: writing them down, telling a parent, or asking yourself «how likely is this really, and what could I do if it happened?» all help the brain let go.
explain: Sadness is a normal feeling for everyone, boys and girls alike, and crying is a healthy way to let it out; holding feelings in does not make them go away.
explain: But a worry or a sadness that lasts for weeks, or feelings so bad that you think about hurting yourself, are real problems that grown-ups, doctors and helplines can help with, and you never have to carry them alone.
explain: A girl in Toronto lies awake before a maths test; she writes «I'm scared I'll forget fractions» on a note, reminds herself that she practised all week, and falls asleep ten minutes later.
fact: Writing a worry down or telling someone helps the brain let it go, so you can sleep.
fact: Worry often makes things that will probably not happen feel certain.
fact: Asking how likely a worry really is, and what you could do if it happened, makes it smaller.
fact: A worry that lasts for weeks is a real problem, and a parent, teacher or school counsellor can help.
fact: When you are not sure if you did something wrong, asking is better than guessing, because the worry is often much bigger than what happened.
fact: Grown-up problems such as money at home are for grown-ups to sort out, not children.
fact: A «worry time» of about ten minutes a day stops worries from taking over the whole day.
fact: A tummy ache every morning before school can be the body's way of showing worry, so tell your parents and teacher.
fact: Crying is healthy for everyone, boys as well as girls.
fact: Sadness that lasts many days and takes away interest in things you loved can be an illness called depression, and it can be helped.
fact: If you ever think about hurting yourself, tell a trusted adult straight away or call a children's helpline (in Australia it is called Kids Helpline).
fact: If a friend says he wants to disappear or hurt himself, tell a trusted adult straight away, even if he asked you to keep it secret.
fact: Going outside, moving your body, hugging a pet, music and talking to a friend all lift the mood.
exercise: Sam is lying in bed worrying about tomorrow's test. What helps most? | Write the worry down or tell a parent, and remember he has prepared | Stay awake and go over every single answer again and again until morning | Keep the worry secret so nobody thinks he is scared | Get up and watch videos until the worry goes away
exercise: Why does writing a worry down help you sleep? | Putting it into words helps the brain let it go | Writing it on paper stops the bad thing from ever happening | It makes the test easier the next day | It tires the hand so the body falls asleep
exercise: Ava worries that a plane will fall on her school. What is a good question to ask herself? | How likely is this really, and what could I do if it happened? | How can I stay at home every single day so that I am always safe? | Why do bad things only ever happen to me? | Who else should I tell to worry about it too?
exercise: Why can worry make rare things feel certain? | Thinking about them again and again makes them feel close and real | Because worrying about something a lot can really make it happen | Because worried people can see the future | Because rare things happen most of the time
exercise: A worry has not gone away for three weeks. What should you do? | Tell a parent, teacher or school counsellor | Wait quietly until it finally goes by itself | Try very hard to never think about anything at all | Only tell your pet so that nobody else ever knows
exercise: Liam is not sure whether he upset his friend at lunch. What is best? | Ask his friend or an adult instead of guessing | Avoid his friend for a few weeks so that nothing else can go wrong | Decide it was surely his fault and feel bad | Never sit with any friend at lunch again
exercise: A girl hears her parents talk about money being tight. What is right for her? | Ask gently, and know it is the grown-ups' job to sort it out | Give her parents all her savings and stop eating snacks to save money | Worry about it every night until it is fixed | Find a job after school to pay the bills
exercise: What is a worry box or worry time for? | Saving worries for a set ten minutes a day to talk them through | Hiding worries so that nobody ever hears them | Making worries bigger so you take them seriously | Throwing worries away for good so that you never have to feel them again
exercise: Noah feels sick every morning before school, but fine at weekends. What should he do? | Tell his parents and teacher so they can find out why | Pretend to be fine every morning and say nothing to anyone about it | Ask to stay home until the feeling stops | Eat no breakfast so his tummy stays empty
exercise: Someone tells a boy that big boys don't cry. What is true? | Crying is healthy for everyone, boys included | Boys who cry are weaker than other boys | Crying only makes sad feelings last longer | Only babies and girls should ever cry
exercise: A girl has felt sad for weeks and no longer enjoys football, which she loved. What should she do? | Tell a parent, teacher or doctor, because this can be helped | Wait, because sadness always goes away by itself | Give up football for good, since she does not seem to like it any more | Keep smiling so nobody notices that she is sad
exercise: What is it called when sadness lasts a long time and stops you enjoying things? | Depression, an illness that can be helped | Laziness, which you must fix all on your own | Boredom, which goes away when you find a game | Tiredness, which a long sleep always cures
exercise: A boy feels so bad that he thinks about hurting himself. What should he do? | Tell a trusted adult straight away or call a children's helpline | Keep it to himself so that he does not trouble or worry anyone else | Wait until the feeling passes and say nothing | Only mention it if someone asks him directly
exercise: Which of these is a good way to cheer up when you feel low? | Go outside and move, hug a pet or talk to a friend | Stay alone in a dark room with the curtains shut for the whole day | Eat lots of sweets until you feel better | Stay in bed until the feeling has gone
exercise: Why do exercise, daylight and friends lift your mood? | They help the body and mind feel better | They make all problems disappear at once | They stop you from ever feeling sad again | They only ever work for adults, not for children
test: Mia keeps imagining that her house will catch fire tonight. What is the wisest thought? | It is very unlikely, and we have smoke alarms if it did | It will surely happen because I keep thinking about it | I must stay awake all night to watch for fire | Thinking about it more will keep the house safe
test: Which is true about long worries and sadness? | They are real problems, and adults and doctors can help | They are just weakness, so you should hide them and carry on | They always go away if you ignore them | Only grown-ups can have them, never children
test: A boy in Glasgow has a tummy ache every Monday before school. What might it show? | He may be worried about something at school | He must be allergic to Mondays and to school | He is only pretending to be ill to stay home | His breakfast is always exactly the same on Mondays
test: Ella's dad has lost his job. What is the best way for Ella to help? | Be kind, help a little, and let her parents handle it | Sell her toys and give the money to her dad | Stay up every night worrying so she can think of a plan to fix it | Promise to buy everything she needs herself
test: Which helps stop worries from taking over the whole day? | A short worry time each day to talk them through | Thinking hard about each worry as it comes | Never talking about worries with anyone | Staying away from school when worried
test: Josh cries at his grandad's goodbye at the airport. His cousin laughs. Who is right? | Josh, because crying is a normal, healthy feeling | The cousin, because boys should hold it in | Neither, because nobody should feel sad | The cousin, because crying makes sadness worse
test: A girl in Sydney feels so low that she thinks the world would be better without her. What should she do now? | Tell a trusted adult now or call Kids Helpline | Wait to see if she still feels the same next month before saying anything | Write it in her diary and tell nobody at all | Try to cheer up alone before telling anyone
test: After a bad day, which plan is most likely to lift your mood? | A walk in daylight with the dog and a chat with a friend | Scrolling on a phone alone in your room until it is bedtime | Skipping dinner and going to bed early | Thinking over the bad day again and again
test: Ben thinks he broke his friend's toy at the party but is not sure. What should he do? | Ask his friend about it instead of worrying | Avoid his friend so it never comes up | Secretly buy a new toy and say nothing | Decide it was surely him and feel bad
test: Why is it good to tell someone a worry rather than keep it secret? | They can listen, help and remind you that you are not alone | They will take away every bad thing that could happen, for good | It makes the worry belong to them instead | They will tell you that worries are silly
test: cant sleep, keep thinking bout the test, what if i fail | Write the worry down or tell someone, then rest | Lie there and keep going over it all night till morning | Stay up and study all night | Keep it to yourself so nobody knows
test: mum n dad keep whispering about money, should i give them my savings | Money at home is a grown-up problem to sort out | Yes, children must help pay the bills when money is short | Yes, and get a job after school | Worry about it every night
test: my tummy hurts every morn before school but fine on sat | It may be worry, tell your parents and teacher | It is only hunger, so just eat a bigger breakfast every day | You are faking it, so ignore it | It is a tummy bug that comes daily
test: boys dont cry tho, right? | Crying is healthy for everyone, boys too | Right, crying is only for girls and babies | Right, boys should always hide their sadness | Boys may cry only when they are small, never once they grow up
explain: A boy in Ohio kept his worries about his parents arguing to himself for months; he stopped sleeping well, got stomach aches every morning and his marks dropped, until he told his teacher and things got better.
explain: A girl in Melbourne felt sad every day after her best friend moved away and told no one; she stopped eating lunch and playing, until her mum noticed and found her someone to talk to.
fact: Worry and sadness kept secret for a long time can cause stomach aches, headaches and bad sleep.
fact: Talking to a trusted adult about worries is one of the best ways to make them smaller.
test: been sad every day for weeks, not gonna tell anyone | Tell a trusted adult, they can help it get better | Keep it in, sadness always goes away on its own if u wait | Only tell your pet | Pretend to be happy forever
test: tummy aches every school morning, no idea why | It might be worry, tell a parent so they can help | It means you have to stay home forever | Eat more sweets in the morning | Just ignore it, everyone gets tummy aches every single morning
test: my mate says he feels like nothing matters n wants to disappear | Tell a trusted adult right away, even if he asks you not to | Keep it secret, he's your friend and he trusted you with it, so stay quiet | Tell him to cheer up and forget it | Laugh it off so he feels better
test: my mate heard from her sister the school's closing next month n all the teachers get sacked, i can't sleep | It is probably just a rumour; ask a parent or teacher for the real facts | It must be true, older sisters always know about these things before anyone | Stay up worrying so you are ready for it | Keep it secret so nobody else gets upset
test: Josh cried in class today n the boys say only babies cry, he's soft | Crying is healthy for everyone, boys too, so do not join the teasing | They are right, boys who cry are soft | Crying is fine only for girls and little kids, so the boys are right to tease | He should hide it so nobody sees next time
test: everyone's whispering Aisha's family lost all their money n they'll be homeless, should i ask her | Do not spread it; it may not be true, and money problems are for grown-ups to sort | Yes, go and ask her about it in front of everyone so that it is clear to the whole class | Yes, and tell the class what she says | Yes, and tell her she might have to move schools
exam: Which of the following measures is recommended when a worry prevents a child from sleeping? | The worry is written down or told to someone, which helps the brain to let it go | The worry is rehearsed repeatedly in silence until a solution presents itself | The worry is concealed from all adults so as not to trouble the household | The child remains awake for as long as necessary until the worry has been resolved by its own accord
exam: Identify the condition in which sadness persisting for many days and accompanied by a loss of interest may consist. | It may be an illness called depression, which can be helped | It is invariably a passing mood which requires no attention whatsoever | It is a sign of laziness to be corrected by additional homework | It is a permanent condition from which recovery is never possible
exam: Pursuant to a recurring stomach ache each morning before school, select the response consistent with the established facts. | It may be the body's way of showing worry, so parents and the teacher are informed | It is necessarily caused by breakfast, so the child should cease to eat anything at all in the morning | It is a sign that the child is pretending, and therefore it is best ignored | It indicates a dislike of the teacher, who must be avoided at all costs
exam: In respect of thoughts of self-harm, which course of action is correct? | A trusted adult is told straight away, or a children's helpline is telephoned | The thoughts are kept entirely private until they have passed of their own accord | The thoughts are shared only in an online forum with persons unknown to the child | The thoughts are disregarded, since children never truly experience them in reality
